  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Isocistanoside C interacts with the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex, which in turn targets Phosphodiesterase 6C. This relationship is supported by multiple pathways, all of which indicate that Isocistanoside C acts as a modulator of the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex, leading to the inhibition of Phosphodiesterase 6C. The consistent scores across the three paths (final_score=0.4637) suggest a strong, reliable interaction, though the exact mechanism of how Isocistanoside C influences the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex remains to be elucidated. The evidence highlights a potential role for Isocistanoside C in regulating the activity of Phosphodiesterase 6C, possibly through a mechanism involving the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex. The findings align with the broader context of drug-target interactions, where modulators of epigenetic regulators can influence downstream enzymes like Phosphodiesterase 6C.",
